// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// Cadence + email preview LAB — data
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// Gulp injects this file into cadence_lab.html on the filename match, so
// its top-level keys (`cadence`, `prospects`) arrive as template
// variables. JSON5, so comments survive.
//
// Lifted from src/views/data/index.json — conv-001's scripted reply
// carries the `cadence` object, and the prospect table above it carries
// the roster. Copied rather than read across for the same reason the
// prospect lab has its own rows: index.json drives the live demo, and a
// draft rewritten here to try a shorter email must not change what the
// conversation page shows.
//
// Re-syncing from index.json is a copy of the same two paths:
//   conversations["conv-001"].scriptedTurns[0].reply[0].cadence
//   conversations["conv-001"].messages[…].table.rows   → id, prospect,
//                                                        role, company,
//                                                        signal
//
// ── The cadence object ────────────────────────────────────────────────
//   id, name             identity, rendered in the header
//   summary              the card-level one-liner after touches/channels;
//                        a sequence can override it with its own `summary`
//   confirmDialog        copy for the Approve-All confirmation
//   completed            copy for the all-approved end state
//   sequences            keyed by prospect id, plus `default`
//
// A sequence is `{ steps: [] }`. A step:
//   type                 email | linkedin | call — picks the rail glyph,
//                        the chip colour, and whether the card shows a
//                        Subject or a Goal line
//   headerLabel/Value    the accordion row's two-part title, rendered
//                        VALUE first ("First touch") then label
//                        ("Email 1") — hence no trailing colon on the
//                        label, which the old order needed
//   day                  which day of the cadence this touch fires,
//                        shown as a "Day n" badge on the row. Days run
//                        front-loaded then widen; the longest sequence
//                        ends on 21 so the cadence summary's "paced
//                        over three weeks" stays true. Omit it and the
//                        badge just doesn't render
//   subject | goal       one line above the body
//   body                 authored markup: <br> for breaks, and
//                        <span class="email-highlight" data-popover-*>
//                        for a citation and its hover card
//   details[]            {title, value} pairs in the Details tab
//   sources[]            {label, icon} chips under them
//   regenerations[]      whole alternate steps, cycled by Regenerate
//
// Tokens `{firstName}` `{prospect}` `{company}` `{signal}` in a subject,
// goal or body are filled per prospect — twice, once by Nunjucks for the
// server-rendered sequence and once by cadence_lab.js after a template
// clone. That's what lets one `default` sequence read as written-for-them
// copy for the 34 prospects with no sequence of their own.
//
// Authored sequences: acct-05-p06 (Alex Bristor — 7 touches, all three
// channels), acct-05-p01 (Natalie Ortega), acct-04-p01 (Max Freeman).
//
// ── The other two keys ────────────────────────────────────────────────
//   prospects   the roster, documented above it
//   cadences    what the Cadence lookup lists, documented above it
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
